div.entryFree:
Arabian bird:phœnix;
                                          fig. unique specimen Ant. III. ii. 12,
                                                Cym. I. vi. 17.

brother(pl. brethren,Tit. I. i. 89348 3 syll. in , 348, 357): often used for ‘brother-in-law’, e.g. Err. II. ii. 156;
                                          also ‘half-brother’ R3
                                                V. iii. 96.

captivatevb.: to take
                                          prisoner, make captive LLL. III. i. 131;
                                          fig. to subjugate, subdue 3H6 I. iv. 115;
                                          to fascinate, charm Ven. 281.

cohere:to agree (withMeas. II. i. 11Tw.N. V. i. 262) , Tw.N. V. i. 262;
                                          so coherence, agreement
                                                2H4 V. i. 72; coherent, in accordance All'sW. III. vii. 39.

confection:compounded
                                          preparation of drugs Cym. I. v. 15;
                                          spec. prepared poison V. v. 247.

derogatevb.: to act in a way
                                          derogatory to one's position Cym. II. i. 50;
                                          in line 59 (quibblingly) degenerate.

erroneous:deviating from the
                                          path of right, criminal 3H6 II. v. 90;
                                          misguided R3 I. iv. 204.

evilsb.2: of uncertain meaning Meas. II. ii. 172, H8 II. i. 67;
                                          interpreted by comm. as ‘jakes, privy’, but
                                          the meaning ‘hovel’ would suit equally well.

facinerious:infamous, vile
                                                All'sW. II. iii. 36 (mod. edd.
                                                facinorous†;
                                          but Latin ‘facinus’ had a byform ‘faciner-’ of the stem ‘facinor-’;
                                          cf. also Old Fr. ‘facinereux’).

fork:only in transferred uses,
                                          of which 1 and 2 are only S.:—(1) forked tongue
                                          (popularly supposed to be the sting) of a snake Meas. III. i. 16, Mac. IV. i. 16;
                                          (2) barbed head of an arrow Lr. I. i. 146;
                                          (3) pl. lower limbs of the body Lr. IV. vi. 122.

forsooth:in truth, certainly;
                                          used by low persons as a phrase of honest asseveration;
                                          implying some contempt when used by well-bred persons
                                          (Schmidt).

gorge:what has been swallowed;
                                                cast the ,Tim. IV. iii. 40 orig. a phrase of
                                          falconry .

div.p:
[guidon†: military flag or pennant, broad at
                                          the end near the staff and forked or pointed at the other;
                                          or the bearer of it H5 IV. ii. 60 (Ff Guard: on).]

div.entryFree:
hastesb.: in ,Mer.V. II. ii. 187180 (1) with speed, quickly, e.g. [180] Return in ,
R2 I. i. 150 In whereof (=‘in order to expedite this proof’);
Lr. II. i. 26 i' the ; so in all ,R3 IV. i. 56 Troil. I. i. 121 In all swift with all possible speed , Troil. I. i. 121 In all swift ; (2) eager to get something done quickly, in a hurry, e.g. Gent. I. iii. 89 He is in ; therefore I pray you, go;—make is freq.; in the same sense are used take his Tim. V. i. 215put it to the hasteAnt. V. ii. 195 , put it to the hasteAnt. V. ii. 195 .

honourable(obs. use): respectable, decent, be coming LLL. V. ii. 328 chides the dice In terms,
Shr. Ind. i. 110 bear himself with action. ¶ The meaning ‘upright, honest’ is not pre-S. All'sW. V. iii. 241, Rom. II. ii. 143, Cæs. III. ii. 88-9; also the advb. use=‘honourably’ 3H6 III. ii. 123 (so F1; Qq Ff2 3 4 honourablyCæs. V. i. 60
), .

least:in the ,Lr. I. i. 194II. iv. 143 (1)
                                          at the lowest estimate ;
                                          (2) in the smallest degree II. iv. 143.

let-alone* (S.): (a) forbearance,
                                          abstention from interference, (hence) permission, (b)
                                          hindrance Lr. V. iii. 80.

lot:prize in a lottery;
                                          allusive phr. lots to
                                          blanks,Cor. V. ii. 10 a thousand to one .

pile:nap of velvet, etc.;
                                          applied to the down on the cheek All'sW. IV. v. 104 (two pile, pile of double the ordinary
                                          closeness).

pip(old edd. peep(e): phr. two-and-thirty, a pip out,Shr. I. ii. 33
                                          not quite the thing ;
                                          ref. to the old card game of one-and-thirty or bone-ace.

prohibit:occurs only once;
                                          used wrongly by Dogberry Ado V. i. 343
                                                [335].

punto:stroke or thrust with
                                          the point of the sword Wiv. II. iii. 26;
                                                 reverso,Rom. II. iv. 28 back-handed thrust .

semblable:adj. similar 2H4
                                                V. i. 72, Ant. III. iv. 3;
                                          sb. (one's) like Tim. IV. iii. 22, Ham. V. ii. 125.

substitute:deputy Meas. V. i. 140, Mer.V. V. i. 94;
                                                by substitute,R3 III. vii. 180 by
                                          proxy .

toasting-iron:toasting-fork;
                                          applied contemptuously to a sword John
                                                IV. iii. 99.

ventvb.: to emit Tp. II. ii. 115, Cym. I. ii. 5;
                                          esp. to utter, e.g. Tp. I. ii. 280;
                                          to void, get rid of 
                                                Cor. I. i. 231
                                                to vent Our musty
                                                  superfluity.

yaw:(of a ship) to move
                                          unsteadily, fig. in Ham. V. ii. 121;
                                          but the passage is difficult.

year:pl.=mature age R2 II. iii. 66 comes to y-s,
2H6 II. iii. 28 a king of y-s;—in y-s, old 1H4 II. iv. 507 [500], Rom. III. v. 46;
LLL. V. ii. 466 smiles his cheek in years (see SMILE 2).

Oui; mettez-le au monpocket;
                                    dépêchez [Fr.]:
                                    Yes; put it in my pocket; be quick Wiv. I. iv. 56.
